Destroy-time cleanup needs to be hardened across core and plugins to prevent leaks and stale async continuations. Destroying and then reusing a WaveSurfer instance for a later load() must still work; cleanup state from the previous lifecycle must not cause future event registrations or renders to be immediately disposed or skipped. Plugins and async code should not touch DOM or internal state after they have been destroyed.

The Spectrogram plugin should release retained audio buffers and fire its destroy event correctly. Its maxCanvasWidth option must be instance-specific rather than shared across all Spectrogram instances. Async spectrogram work must check whether the plugin has been destroyed after awaits and avoid accessing cleared state. The windowed spectrogram variant must stop re-arming timers after destroy and should enforce its retained segment cap during progressive loading.

The Envelope plugin should not accumulate duplicate subscriptions or drag handlers when its polyline/points are replaced or updated. Per-point drag streams and listeners should be disposed when the point/polyline lifecycle ends.

The Record plugin should stop recording during teardown, destroy its timer, tolerate re-initialization, and still deliver the final record-end event to listeners registered at destroy time even if the underlying MediaRecorder.onstop callback fires asynchronously after destroy() returns.

The Regions plugin should clean up drag-selection disposal, avoid accumulating duplicate content listener pairs, and correctly clean up resize toggles. Timeline and Hover should clear any notch/transition caches and avoid duplicate transitionend listeners after destroy. Minimap should unsubscribe its event bridge before destroying its nested WaveSurfer instance.

A new Scope disposal-tree primitive is expected for internal lifecycle ownership. It should support idempotent disposal, child scopes, adding disposer callbacks, disposing children before parent-owned disposers, LIFO disposal for callbacks owned by the same scope, and immediate execution of disposers added after the scope has already been disposed. Shared observer cleanup should unobserve only the relevant element rather than disconnecting a shared observer used by other elements.

Several unused internal modules should no longer be treated as maintained source modules: src/draggable.ts, src/reactive/event-stream-emitter.ts, src/reactive/media-event-bridge.ts, src/reactive/render-scheduler.ts, and src/reactive/state-event-emitter.ts. Existing code should use the current reactive/drag-stream and event-stream utilities instead, and documentation should no longer describe deleted modules or inaccurate automatic batching behavior. The reactive README should document the actual batch(), disposable computed()/dispose(), auto-tracking, and Scope behavior.
